 1.⁠ ⁠Introduction to Computer Hardware

Goal
To introduce the three main subsystems of a computer before studying them separately.

1.1 Main Computer Subsystems
If we divide a computer into parts, we will get three broad categories or subsystems which make a computer work. These are the central processing unit (CPU), the main memory, and the input/output subsystem. The next sections discuss these subsystems and how they are connected.
The CPU processes instructions and performs calculations. Main memory holds data and instructions that are currently needed. The input/output subsystem allows the computer to receive information, show results, and communicate with other devices.
For example, when we type a number on a keyboard, the computer receives it through an input device. The CPU can process the number, and the result can be shown on a monitor. During this process, memory holds the information the computer is using.

1.2 How the Subsystems Work Together
The three subsystems have different roles, but none of them can do everything alone. A CPU needs instructions and data from memory. A user also needs input and output devices to interact with a computer.

Diagram(Keyboard → CPU ↔️ Main Memory → Monitor. )

